// The control for the fixtures either side: a prelude type that IS built.
//
// Materialising one has always put its declaration in the output, so this shape
// agreed before the named-but-never-built case was mirrored and has to keep
// agreeing after it. It is the half of the property that was never broken, and
// pinning only the half that was would let a fix trade one half for the other -
// a rule written to catch the named case can lose the built one by taking over
// its job and getting the fields, or the position, differently.
//
// The user enum keeps the collision in the picture, so this is the built twin of
// the silent-kind fixture rather than a program that merely uses an allocator.
// It is also the sharpest form of the assertion: the C companion beside this
// file lays out the allocator by hand, so if the emitted declaration were the
// user's two-variant enum the two layouts would disagree and the program would
// read the wrong memory at run time rather than only looking wrong.

enum Allocator { A | B }

pub mod raw { extern fn make_c_allocator() -> Allocator; }

fn takes(x: *const Allocator) { println(1); }

fn main() {
    let a = raw::make_c_allocator();
    let mut v: Vec<int> = Vec::with_capacity(a, 8 as u32);
    v.push(7);
    v.push(35);
    println(v.length() as int);   // 2
    println(v[1] - v[0]);         // 28
}
